title: "The Constitution as a Founding Inscription: What Kind of Social Object a National Constitution Is, and How It Institutionalizes the Human Condition Across the World's Legal Traditions"
authors:
  - family-names: Bourguet
    given-names: Luis
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4673-4486"
    affiliation: "LabOnt, Universita di Torino; CSAL, Universita di Bologna"
abstract: "This article brings together two questions usually kept apart: the anthropological-philosophical question of the human condition and the ontological-legal question of what kind of social object a national constitution is. The thesis merges both: a constitution is a founding inscription, a sovereign, recursive, generative and maximally unamendable social object, operating as the mechanism through which a political community stabilizes its answers to the human condition."
